    "title": "How defense led Ole Miss women's basketball March Madness win vs Gonzaga",
    "text": "March 20, 2026, 5:54 p.m. CTThe Ole Miss women's basketball team defeated Gonzaga 81-66 in the first round of the NCAA Tournament.Ole Miss's defense held Gonzaga to 36.5% shooting and forced 21 turnovers.Four Ole Miss players scored in double figures, led by Latasha Lattimore with 15 points.The Rebels advance to the second round to face either Minnesota or Green Bay.MINNEAPOLIS — Ole Miss women's basketball is off to a strong start in the 2026 NCAA Tournament.The No. 5 seed Rebels (24-11) took down No. 12 seed Gonzaga (24-10) in the first round, beating the Bulldogs 81-66 at Williams Arena on March 20.It was the second time under coach Yolett McPhee-McCuin that Ole Miss beat Gonzaga in the first round of March Madness, also doing so in 2023.With the win, the Rebels will move on to face No. 4 seed Minnesota or No. 13 seed Green Bay in the second round on March 22.Ole Miss dominated Gonzaga for three quarters. The Rebels held the Bulldogs to single digits in the first quarter and outscored them by a total of 20 points in the middle two periods. Thanks to a 29-point third quarter, Ole Miss led by 32 points heading into the final quarter.Ole Miss used its defense to get on top of Gonzaga and hold onto the lead for the entire game. The Bulldogs entered the game shooting 45.4% from the field, and Ole Miss held them to 36.5%.The Rebels forced the Bulldogs into committing 21 turnovers, resulting in 23 points for Ole Miss. 14 of those turnovers were Ole Miss steals. Five Rebels finished the game with multiple steals. Ole Miss also protected the the rim, totaling nine blocks.Lauren Whittaker, the WCC Freshman and Player of the Year shot just 4-for-21 and finished with eight points, 13 rebounds and six turnovers.Ole Miss saw four players score in double-figures. Latasha Lattimore led the team with 15 points, Cotie McMahon added 13, and Denim Deshields scored 11.Sira Thienou, playing in her first game since Feb. 17 because of injury, finished with 12 points, eight rebounds, four blocks and three steals.Despite Ole Miss' strong defensive showing and balanced scoring, Gonzaga managed to close the gap in the fourth quarter, outscoring the Rebels by 16 points in the period.Allie Turner, the game's leading scorer, recorded 11 of her 27 points in the final quarter. On the day, Turner shot 7-for-12 from the field and 5-for-9 from the 3-point line.Gonzaga managed to cut Ole Miss' lead to 10 points with 49 seconds left, but Ole Miss held on to close out the game and move on in the tournament.Tia Reid covers Jackson State sports for the Clarion Ledger. Email her at treid@usatodayco.com and follow her on X @tiareid65.",
